    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d416">

                <pb facs="0416" n="406" xml:id="pg406"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 E.W.1939/921</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Adviser on Political Relations</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Chief of the Division of European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Atherton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-04-30">April 30, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Atherton</hi>: The Secretary this afternoon said
                    that the President had asked him to have drafted a form of announcement the
                    President might make with respect to the use of poison gas by any of our
                    enemies. He preferred not to take any action with respect to the reported threat
                    of the use of poison gas by the Germans against the Russians such as the
                    announcement in that respect made by Prime Minister Churchill of Great Britain
                    on April 21 last, but he would prefer to have any declaration or announcement he
                    would make apply to any enemy situation where poison gas might be employed.</p>
                <p>I don’t know just whom you might choose to start the draft on such a
                    pronouncement.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James Clement Dunn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
